Shor's algorithm is
typically implemented using Python with specialized quantum computing frameworks such as Qiskit or PennyLane. These libraries provide the necessary tools to build and simulate quantum circuits or run them on real quantum hardware. 
Which Language to Use
Python is the primary and most popular language for implementing quantum algorithms due to its user-friendliness and extensive library support. 

    Qiskit: Developed by IBM, this open-source framework is excellent for building quantum circuits and running experiments on local simulators or connecting to real IBM Quantum computers via an API token.
    PennyLane: This is a quantum machine learning library that allows for hybrid classical-quantum workflows and just-in-time compilation of circuits.
    PyQuil: Developed by Rigetti, this library uses the Quil instruction set and can run on real Quantum Processing Units (QPUs).
    Q#: Microsoft provides its own quantum programming language, which is part of the Azure Quantum development kit. 

For simulating Shor's algorithm on a classical computer, Python (with libraries like NumPy) is recommended. The memory and processing requirements for simulating large numbers grow exponentially, so practical simulations are limited to factoring small numbers like 15 or 21. 
